<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to the BlockML expression language

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.Expressions-->
<docs:Expressions xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:mapping="org.blockml.bml.mapping"
  xmlns:service="org.blockml.bml.service">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to the BlockML expression language
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: the BlockML expression language, dollar-brace expressions, their evaluation context,
    and Text interpolation holes, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.Expressions. Body
    content is authored in a03 from that source.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:Expressions" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — expression grammar, #this lookup, and interpolation classification.
    </see>
    <see type="mapping:FunctionMapping" relation="SeeAlso">
      Call-argument mapping used at expression call sites.
    </see>
    <see type="service:Math" relation="SeeAlso">
      Built-in #math.* service used inside expressions.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Expressions</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <!--1. Lead-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Expressions as dynamic member values">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>An expression is a small piece of syntax written inside dollar-brace
delimiters that lets a member's value be computed or derived instead of written
as a fixed literal. An expression can appear wherever a member's value needs to
be dynamic, computed, or bound to a service, rather than authored as a plain
literal.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--2. What it can reference-->
        <dpage:Section heading="What an expression can reference">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Beyond ordinary literal values, an expression can refer to another
member of the same Block instance by name, or call a built-in utility such as
the math service to compute a result. A call to a built-in or custom capability
can pass its arguments either by position or by name, following the same
declared order used elsewhere in BlockML. Built-in numeric utilities such as
minimum, maximum, absolute value, rounding, power, and square root are
available directly inside an expression through the math service.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--3. Evaluation context-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Evaluation context">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>An expression is always evaluated against the Block instance that owns
it, so a property reference always resolves within that same instance rather
than reaching into a parent or sibling.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--4. Interpolation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Interpolation inside text">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A text value may also weave one or more restricted expression holes
into ordinary running text, and BlockML classifies that case as interpolation
rather than as a single whole expression value. Only a value that consists
entirely of one dollar-brace expression, with nothing else around it, is
treated as a whole expression value; a plain string with no holes at all is a
plain literal.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Because an interpolation hole sits inside ordinary text, it allows
only a limited subset of the expression language — enough to reference a
property value or call a small set of built-in utilities, but not enough to
bind to a model path, call a bean, or use the loop and scoping variables
available inside a whole expression.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--5. Recap-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Recognizing an expression at a glance">
          <content>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="A whole value wrapped entirely in dollar braces is evaluated as a full expression." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Dollar-brace holes woven into a longer text value are evaluated as interpolation." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A value with no dollar braces at all is a plain literal, used exactly as written." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--6. Continuation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with value configuration">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Value Configuration" summary="Learn curly-brace configuration of a Property's owned type instance.">
                  <target>docs:ValueConfiguration</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:Expressions>
